## Repository Structure

```text
.
├── datasets/
│   ├── prompt_sets/
│   │   ├── single_prompts.json
│   │   ├── multi_turn_conversations.json
│   │   └── distractor_prompts.json
│   ├── combined_pirate_dataset.json
│   └── pirate_refs.txt
├── prompt_tests.py
├── pirate_score_json.py
├── cosine_style_score.py
├── analyze_cosine.py
├── analyze_scores.py
├── plot_cosine.py
├── train_pirate_bert.py
├── score_bert.py
├── plot_bert.py
├── make_pirate_patch.py
├── extract_pirate_refs.py
└── (course-provided training/model scripts)
What Each Directory / File Is For
datasets/

Contains all evaluation and training data used in the project.

datasets/prompt_sets/single_prompts.json: 30 single-turn prompts

datasets/prompt_sets/multi_turn_conversations.json: 30 multi-turn conversations

datasets/prompt_sets/distractor_prompts.json: 30 distractor/conflict prompts

datasets/pirate_refs.txt: pirate reference sentences used for cosine similarity scoring

datasets/combined_pirate_dataset.json: labeled data used to train the BERT classifier

Top-level scripts

Contains the training, scoring, analysis, and plotting scripts used throughout the project.

Environment Setup

We provide a pyproject.toml file for reproducible installation.

Option A: Poetry (recommended)
poetry install
poetry shell
Option B: pip

Install the main dependencies manually:

pip install torch datasets transformers sentence-transformers scikit-learn pandas matplotlib
Reproducibility Notes

For best reproducibility, dependency versions should be pinned in pyproject.toml or another environment file such as requirements.txt or environment.yml.

Model checkpoint files are not committed to GitHub because .pth files are too large for the repository.

Expected local paths:

Baseline chat model: tinystories_chat_model/best_model.pth

Patched chat model: tinystories_chat_model_patched/best_model.pth

If external checkpoint sharing is needed, add a link here:

Checkpoint link: [Add checkpoint link here]

Example Usage

A typical workflow is:

python prompt_tests.py
python cosine_style_score.py
python analyze_cosine.py
python plot_cosine.py
python train_pirate_bert.py
python score_bert.py
python plot_bert.py

This runs prompt evaluation, computes cosine similarity scores, trains the BERT style classifier, scores outputs with the classifier, and generates plots.

Step-by-Step Reproduction
1. Run Prompt Stress Tests

This script runs the three prompt sets and saves generations.

python prompt_tests.py
Output

results_all_tests.json

To switch between baseline and patched models, update the checkpoint path inside prompt_tests.py unless you extend the script with command-line arguments.

2. Compute Cosine Similarity Scores

This computes embedding cosine similarity against pirate reference texts in datasets/pirate_refs.txt.

python cosine_style_score.py
Typical outputs

results_all_tests_scored.csv

results_all_tests_summary.csv

3. Aggregate and Inspect Scores
python analyze_cosine.py
python analyze_scores.py

These scripts summarize scores and help inspect category-level behavior.

4. Plot Cosine Similarity Results
python plot_cosine.py

This generates figures for cosine-based evaluation.

5. Train the BERT Style Classifier

The classifier is trained on datasets/combined_pirate_dataset.json.

python train_pirate_bert.py

This produces a saved classifier model directory, depending on the script configuration.

6. Score Generations with the BERT Classifier

This estimates:

P(pirate_style | generated_text)

for each model response.

python score_bert.py
7. Plot BERT Results
python plot_bert.py

This produces classifier-based result plots.

8. Generate a Pirate Patch Dataset
python make_pirate_patch.py
Expected output

pirate_patch.jsonl
(filename may vary depending on script settings)

9. Continue Fine-Tuning the Chat Model

Using the course-provided training script:

python train_tinystories_chat_model.py \
  --pretrained_model_path tinystories_chat_model/best_model.pth \
  --dataset_path pirate_patch.jsonl \
  --output_dir tinystories_chat_model_patched

After creating the patched checkpoint, repeat Steps 1–7 and compare baseline vs patched results.

Documented Scripts

The main scripts in this repository are:

prompt_tests.py: runs all prompt sets and saves generations

pirate_score_json.py: quick keyword-based scoring sanity check

cosine_style_score.py: computes embedding cosine similarity to pirate references

analyze_cosine.py: aggregates cosine results by prompt category

analyze_scores.py: inspects scored outputs from CSV/JSON

plot_cosine.py: plots cosine-similarity results

train_pirate_bert.py: trains the BERT pirate-style classifier

score_bert.py: scores generations with the trained classifier

plot_bert.py: plots BERT classifier results

make_pirate_patch.py: generates the pirate patch dataset

extract_pirate_refs.py: creates or updates pirate references / classifier data

Important note on script interfaces

If your course requires argparse or click command-line interfaces with --help text, only claim that if your scripts actually implement them. If they currently do not, the honest phrasing is:

Scripts are currently run directly by editing paths/configs in the source files. Adding argparse-based command-line interfaces is a natural next improvement for reproducibility.

Output Management

The main outputs generated by this project are:

results_all_tests.json: raw generations from stress tests

*_scored.csv: per-example scoring outputs

*_summary.csv: category-level summary statistics

plot files from plot_cosine.py and plot_bert.py

model folders produced by train_pirate_bert.py and patch fine-tuning

Depending on the script settings, outputs may be saved either in the project root or in a results/model directory.

Regenerating All Figures and Tables

To reproduce the main analysis artifacts:

Run prompt_tests.py

Run cosine_style_score.py

Run train_pirate_bert.py and score_bert.py

Run analyze_cosine.py, analyze_scores.py, plot_cosine.py, and plot_bert.py

